{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_6ggcuqtnyaol4y2yngdq5hkgp37fvuafxn5e6ca6kfvxifzvk6da","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and the completion gate passes. Summary:\n\n## What I did\n\n**Phase A — artifacts** (all in `translation/`, all mechanically derived from `c_src/`):\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — all 79 C dynamic symbols (78 from `libcjson.so` + `driver` from `libcJSON_test.so`) tabulated against the Rust `.so`. Diff is **empty**; 0 undefined non-libc symbols. The one extra Rust export (`cJSON_Duplicate_rec`) is explained: it has external linkage in `cJSON.c` but no `CJSON_PUBLIC`, so `-fvisibility=hidden` hides it on the C side.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — **197 rows**, one per distinct rejection, derived by grepping all 169 `return false` / `return NULL` / `return 0` / `goto fail` sites and deduplicating. 159 rows have a named `err_*` test, 21 need a failing allocator (`hooks.rs`), 17 are dead code with the dominating check named, 1 (row 56) is provably unprovokable with an explanation.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — **80 rows**, all checked, each naming its test. Axes derived from the source: the 6 `cJSON_InitHooks` allocator configurations (which select two completely different buffer-growth paths), 4 print × 4 parse entry points × their option cross-product, all 9 value types ± `IsReference`/`StringIsConst`, number/string/container shapes, and the C-side CMake options.\n\n**Phases B/C/D** — 123 tests in 10 binaries; both `.so`s loaded via `libloading`, no Rust function ever called directly. Verified under `release` **and** `debug` (debug enables overflow checks) × both feature invocations by `verify.sh`, which also re-diffs symbols and audits the three artifacts for internal consistency.\n\n## Bug found and fixed\n\n`cJSON_GetNumberValue` returned the wrong NaN. `cJSON.c` is compiled `-std=c89`, so glibc leaves `NAN` undefined and the library's own `#define NAN 0.0/0.0` applies — which GCC folds to the **negative** default quiet NaN `0xFFF8000000000000`, not Rust's `f64::NAN` (`0x7FF8...`). Fixed with an explicit `C_NAN` constant.\n\n## Making the verification trustworthy\n\nPassing tests prove little on their own, so I mutation-tested the suite (`mutation_check.py`, results in `MUTATION.md`): 62 injected defects, **53 killed**. Each of the 9 survivors has an equivalence proof (e.g. `parse_number`'s `>=` vs `>` at `INT_MAX` produces the same `valueint` either way).\n\nTwo survivors were real blind spots, and closing them required going outside output comparison — a missing `deallocate` and a one-byte-short allocation change nothing observable. Since `cJSON_InitHooks` hands the allocator to the caller, I added `tests/guarded.rs`: canary header/footer per block plus alloc/free accounting, requiring C and Rust to make the *same* allocator calls. That killed the leak mutant and independently proved the off-by-one survivor is safe (`allocation_length` already counts the opening quote). Allocator counting also made `cJSON_InitHooks`'s `reallocate` selection observable, which is otherwise invisible.\n\nTwo further notes worth flagging:\n- **ERRORS rows 22/24/25** need an output above `INT_MAX/2`. Rather than declaring them unreachable, `tests/bigalloc.rs` reaches them with a ~358 MB `cJSON_CreateStringReference` payload (control bytes cost 6× when escaped) and, for row 25, temporarily lowers `RLIMIT_AS` so the ~2 GiB `realloc` genuinely fails.\n- The debug profile initially **stack-overflowed** on the 10 000-deep `cJSON_Duplicate` recursion. This is not a logic divergence — C at `-O0` uses an 80-byte frame, release Rust 64 bytes, debug Rust 320 bytes — so that probe now runs on a thread with an explicit large stack, with the reasoning documented in the test.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the task asked me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1023,"cache_read_input_tokens":524760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1023,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f12580f7-38a2-49ac-9a6e-10804310ebb6","uuid":"d3a5ebd8-5ead-40eb-89ca-46caf3849b98","timestamp":"2026-08-27T07:08:30.487Z"}
